WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.820
Hi, I'm Sohan.

2
00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.800
This is Procurement Approvals,

3
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.570
a proof of concept I built on Microsoft Power Platform.

4
00:00:07.832 --> 00:00:09.672
It takes a purchase order from request

5
00:00:09.672 --> 00:00:12.772
to approval without one forwarded email.

6
00:00:13.043 --> 00:00:17.413
In a lot of companies, a purchase order still travels by email.

7
00:00:17.672 --> 00:00:21.932
It goes to the manager, then finance, then procurement,

8
00:00:21.932 --> 00:00:24.322
and nobody can see where it's stuck.

9
00:00:24.597 --> 00:00:26.237
Here's the app as Abdullah,

10
00:00:26.237 --> 00:00:29.097
an IT engineer who needs to buy something.

11
00:00:29.356 --> 00:00:32.596
He sees his own orders, what's waiting in approval,

12
00:00:32.596 --> 00:00:35.166
and the spend approved so far this year.

13
00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:40.615
He picks the vendor.

14
00:00:40.615 --> 00:00:44.165
Only vendors that procurement has approved are on this list.

15
00:00:44.431 --> 00:00:47.411
He enters 61,800 riyals,

16
00:00:47.411 --> 00:00:51.631
and the panel on the right shows the route straight away.

17
00:00:51.893 --> 00:00:55.193
Three approvals, because it's over 50,000.

18
00:00:55.193 --> 00:00:58.513
A short justification, and he submits.

19
00:00:58.782 --> 00:01:01.202
A Power Automate flow takes over.

20
00:01:01.202 --> 00:01:04.342
It checks the order, opens the first approval step,

21
00:01:04.342 --> 00:01:06.082
and emails the manager.

22
00:01:07.418 --> 00:01:10.718
For the demo, I can act as each person.

23
00:01:10.718 --> 00:01:12.978
This is Khalid, the IT manager.

24
00:01:13.238 --> 00:01:16.018
He approves from the order itself, with a comment,

25
00:01:16.018 --> 00:01:21.098
and a second flow moves it on to finance within seconds.

26
00:01:24.855 --> 00:01:26.835
Huda, the finance director,

27
00:01:26.835 --> 00:01:29.735
sees a budget check the flow wrote for her.

28
00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:32.387
How much IT has already spent this year,

29
00:01:32.387 --> 00:01:34.687
and where this order would take it.

30
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.520
Turki in procurement signs off last,

31
00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.180
and the order is fully approved.

32
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:47.767
A final flow emails Abdullah and books a processing slot

33
00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:50.307
on the next Saudi working day.

34
00:01:50.565 --> 00:01:55.265
Every step lands in the audit trail: who did what, and when,

35
00:01:55.265 --> 00:01:56.965
in Riyadh time.

36
00:02:01.168 --> 00:02:03.868
The vendor list keeps the commercial registration,

37
00:02:03.868 --> 00:02:06.578
the VAT number and a risk level.

38
00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:10.489
High-risk vendors get a warning on every approval.

39
00:02:11.373 --> 00:02:13.753
Approvers aren't always at a desk,

40
00:02:13.753 --> 00:02:16.653
so the same app works on a phone.

41
00:02:16.909 --> 00:02:19.359
Under the hood it's six Dataverse tables,

42
00:02:19.359 --> 00:02:23.669
one canvas app and four flows. No custom code.

43
00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:27.433
Everything here is sample data in a developer environment,

44
00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:30.303
but the flow times you saw are real.

45
00:02:30.577 --> 00:02:34.217
The full build, the tests and the scripts are on my GitHub,

46
00:02:34.217 --> 00:02:37.627
and there's more on my portfolio. Thanks for watching.
